#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c07e202-40b7-5508-af3d-6a272ed0cbc2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21eafcc3-ef61-5480-b57c-348255ceb796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333293} {:answer/id #uuid "49290232-5081-5b5d-bd61-6db73b6ba662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333663} {:answer/id #uuid "9b48e57b-80c2-5c26-8e33-43322de288da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334360} {:answer/id #uuid "9d78a1c6-84d9-5a1b-a149-8bda9c0c8dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334377} {:answer/id #uuid "c061b078-1635-5eed-8151-acb424baec86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49290232-5081-5b5d-bd61-6db73b6ba662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333663} :answer-field/id #uuid "db803abe-1f8c-5957-9dd0-b0fc75fe341c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a70227b-6237-55b5-b378-b7662770487e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64228" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm 3\\text{i},$ and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+3x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ {y}^{″} & =6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -6\\alpha =-12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+x(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3P\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+x(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3Q+2=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P=2,Q=1.$\n\nSo, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c07e202-40b7-5508-af3d-6a272ed0cbc2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2917b253-31dc-5dfb-9496-fbebe10e444f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348801} {:answer/id #uuid "70ddbda2-de76-5999-9f43-d08b9cd03988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6{e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349485} {:answer/id #uuid "ab129a58-cc2e-5fa4-8273-2b53da11d95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6x{e}^{-x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349964} {:answer/id #uuid "be3139ce-6b6a-56fb-8727-cf1ab160e969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6{e}^{-x}+12x{e}^{-x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350137} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c4a254-8696-5e1c-b8e8-4e4596f7d784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}+6x{e}^{-x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab129a58-cc2e-5fa4-8273-2b53da11d95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6x{e}^{-x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349964} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f33e624-4874-5a1f-a88f-20565a7ae994" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f684abde-2923-5a1b-8719-a76da5c1d85c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8114" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,\\,y(0)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda +1)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(-4\\alpha -3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ -4\\alpha -3\\beta =-25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =4$ and $\\beta =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4 & =4 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-x}-Bx{e}^{-x}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-A+B+6 & =0 \\\\ B & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=0,B=-6.$\n\nSo, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-6x{e}^{-x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e77e8eb-4f76-565b-b3a2-5c91767ba39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404442} {:answer/id #uuid "de87823a-03c0-572e-9778-3d0e131e7ba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404443} {:answer/id #uuid "edee0d23-5e90-53e9-9a9b-3e624f83441e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404444} {:answer/id #uuid "45a9656a-bcf9-5ffd-9994-d663146b2ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404445} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c913bf-1bbb-5e26-96bd-2c1ec496da34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de87823a-03c0-572e-9778-3d0e131e7ba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404443} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dec29b5-6835-594f-89d3-a76d54e5866b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe1794bb-4b33-510b-8172-929bfa639d36" :question/math-academy-id "q-8854" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+4x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n8(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)+16x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+16x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\,=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-8\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+8\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\ -8\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2}$, $\\beta =0.$\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ff3d06-b078-5717-9490-3faff9c71641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487577} {:answer/id #uuid "1558b0b5-c4b1-5e89-a0e8-d7774ed107d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487612} {:answer/id #uuid "300772cd-f5ba-520d-8474-76518d72ebc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487657} {:answer/id #uuid "db054387-992a-574d-a0fb-723173337d03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487980} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1ead3d-19b8-5d1a-aaf4-197e07495f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "300772cd-f5ba-520d-8474-76518d72ebc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487657} :answer-field/id #uuid "1132dd4f-2625-5c46-a672-de1c19731cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b54ddcc0-5401-5d92-820d-ec32389dd88e" :question/math-academy-id "q-93821" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & =2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -2\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =0$ and $\\beta =-2.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c07e202-40b7-5508-af3d-6a272ed0cbc2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e5c9b1-43df-5f7d-aecb-00237a87f249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355092} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd0492c-a6f4-5e47-a8d3-5f8f8c570225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355234} {:answer/id #uuid "83c85d82-9f4d-5dcd-9620-806a3fb14661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}-4x{e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355825} {:answer/id #uuid "921b7f74-7ba0-5166-ae5f-4fc5433f8be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{-2x}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355955} {:answer/id #uuid "e1145164-af87-5e30-966d-17507213ec37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1145164-af87-5e30-966d-17507213ec37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356635} :answer-field/id #uuid "f43be71d-0862-510f-b914-b36421c29d54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50d3f5fe-3c9a-51a8-8637-85b029f95cf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-93833" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,\\,y(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =A{e}^{-2(0)}+B(0){e}^{-2(0)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(0) \\\\ -1 & =A-2 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}-2Bx{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-2$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =-2A{e}^{-2(0)}-2B(0){e}^{-2(0)}+B{e}^{-2(0)}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2(0) \\\\ -2 & =-2A+B \\\\ -2 & =-2+B \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=1$ and $B=0,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$"}]}
